---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Cite from '../components/Cite.astro';
import VoteSlider from '../components/VoteSlider.astro';
import CapCalculator from '../components/CapCalculator.astro';
import SourceList from '../components/SourceList.astro';
import ShareRow from '../components/ShareRow.astro';
import ThemeToggle from '../components/ThemeToggle.astro';
import SectionMenu from '../components/SectionMenu.astro';
import ScoreChart from '../components/ScoreChart.astro';
import SpendChart from '../components/SpendChart.astro';
import { site, calendarEvent } from '../data/site';
import { scores, scoresByGrade, scoreYearsByGrade, spendingCategories } from '../data/figures';

// Deep links into the Oct. 19, 2026 Farmington School Board video.
const vid = (seconds: number) => `https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qcF6xnfGMWc&t=${seconds}s`;

// Spending by category: dollars in $ thousands, change before and after inflation.
const cpiRatio = spendingCategories.cpi2024 / spendingCategories.cpi2016;
const thousands = (v: number) => (v >= 1000 ? `$${(Math.round(v / 10) / 100).toFixed(2)}M` : `$${v}K`);
const signed = (v: number) => `${v > 0 ? '+' : v < 0 ? '−' : ''}${Math.abs(v).toFixed(0)}%`;
const spendRow = (r: { fy2016: number; fy2024: number }) => ({
  nominal: signed((r.fy2024 / r.fy2016 - 1) * 100),
  real: signed((r.fy2024 / r.fy2016 / cpiRatio - 1) * 100),
});

// "Add to Google Calendar" for Election Day (an all-day event). The .ics version is src/pages/vote-question-2.ics.ts.
const googleCalendar = `https://calendar.google.com/calendar/render?${new URLSearchParams({
  action: 'TEMPLATE',
  text: calendarEvent.title,
  dates: `${site.electionDateISO.replaceAll('-', '')}/${calendarEvent.endISO.replaceAll('-', '')}`,
  details: `${calendarEvent.details}\n\n${new URL('/', Astro.site)}`,
})}`;

const ballotText = {
  before:
    '“Shall the Town of FARMINGTON limit property tax growth for FARMINGTON SCHOOL DISTRICT (SAU #61) under RSA 32:5-i? If adopted for a two-year period: (1) the local property tax levy may not grow beyond the prior year’s amount, adjusted for inflation and new construction; (2) SAU central office spending may not exceed 6 percent of total school district appropriations; and (3) bonded capital costs are excluded from both limits. ',
  marked:
    'These caps apply only to administrative operations of the SAU central office and do not affect classroom instruction, school-based services, or other municipal expenditures.',
  after: ' These limits may be overridden as provided in RSA 32:5-i. Adoption requires a three-fifths (3/5) majority vote.”',
};
---
<Base>
  <header class="topbar" slot="header">
    <div class="wrap">
      <a class="brand" href="#top">
        <svg width="26" height="26" viewBox="0 0 64 64" aria-hidden="true"><rect width="64" height="64" rx="12" fill="#14325c"/><path d="M14 46V18h22v6H21v5h13v6H21v11z" fill="#fff"/><circle cx="46" cy="40" r="7" fill="#f2b33d"/></svg>
        <span>Farmington &amp; Question 2</span>
      </a>
      <nav class="topnav" aria-label="Main sections">
        <a href="#farmington">Finances</a>
        <a href="#outcomes">Outcomes</a>
        <a href="#community">Families</a>
        <a href="#ballot">The law</a>
        <a href="#alternatives">Alternatives</a>
        <a href="#vote">How to vote</a>
      </nav>
      <SectionMenu />
      <ThemeToggle />
    </div>
  </header>

  <!-- ===================== HERO ===================== -->
  <section class="hero" id="top">
    <div class="wrap hero-grid">
      <div>
        <p class="date-pill"><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>{site.electionDateShort} · Question 2 is on the back of your ballot</p>
        <h1>It’s called a “tax cap.”<br /> It’s really a <em>school cap.</em></h1>
        <p class="lede">
          Question 2 won’t cap your property tax bill. It caps what Farmington can raise in property taxes for its
          schools, the money behind teachers, buses, and special education, not just administration. District leaders
          estimate that leaves about <strong>$360,000 short</strong> in the first year.
          <Cite id="nhjournal-poll" /> <Cite id="school-board-video" href={vid(2772)} label="School Board, Oct. 19" />
        </p>
        <p class="byline">
          {site.publisher && <>I’m {site.publisher}, and </>}<strong>I recommend voting No on Question 2.</strong> Here’s why,
          in plain English, with a source for every fact and the other side’s best arguments.
        </p>
        <div class="cta-row">
          <a class="btn primary" href="#quick">Read the 1-minute version</a>
          <a class="btn ghost" href="#calculator">Try the numbers</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <aside class="glance card" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
        <h2 id="glance-title" class="kicker">Question 2 at a glance</h2>
        <dl>
          <div><dt>2</dt><dd>caps in one yes-or-no question: one on the school property-tax levy, one on administration</dd></div>
          <div><dt>60%</dt><dd>“yes” needed to adopt the cap, and again whenever a budget would need taxes above it</dd></div>
          <div><dt>$360K</dt><dd>first-year gap district leaders estimate at 3% inflation, before special ed, fuel, and transportation. On the state’s tax figures, it could be closer to $500K (my math).</dd></div>
          <div><dt>5.7%</dt><dd>Farmington’s central-office share by the district’s count, about $47,000 under the 6% limit. The levy cap is the part that would bite.</dd></div>
        </dl>
        <p class="small muted" style="margin:0">
          <Cite id="rsa-32-5-i" label="The law" /> <Cite id="school-board-video" href={vid(2772)} label="School Board, Oct. 19" />
          <Cite id="school-board-video" href={vid(3564)} label="59:24" /> <Cite id="school-board-video" href={vid(3749)} label="1:02:29" />
          <Cite id="town-report-2025" label="2025 tax rate sheet" />
        </p>
      </aside>
    </div>
    <div class="wrap">
      <nav class="jump" aria-labelledby="jump-title">
        <h2 id="jump-title" class="jump-title">On this page</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><a href="#quick">5 things to know</a></li>
          <li><a href="#calculator">The $360K gap</a></li>
          <li><a href="#spending">Spending vs. inflation</a></li>
          <li><a href="#outcomes">Test scores</a></li>
          <li><a href="#community">Families and taxes</a></li>
          <li><a href="#ballot">The ballot wording</a></li>
          <li><a href="#sixty">The 60% rule</a></li>
          <li><a href="#other-side">Supporters’ case</a></li>
          <li><a href="#alternatives">Alternatives</a></li>
          <li><a href="#vote">How to vote</a></li>
          <li><a href="#faq">FAQ</a></li>
        </ul>
      </nav>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ===================== QUICK ===================== -->
  <section id="quick" class="alt">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">The 1-minute version</p>
      <h2>Five things to know before you vote</h2>
      <ol class="steps prose">
        <li>
          <p>
            <strong>It caps the school district’s property-tax levy, not just administration.</strong> Question 2
            bundles two limits into one vote: central-office administration at 6% of the budget, and the district’s
            whole local property-tax levy (minus building-loan payments), which helps pay for teachers, buses, heat, and
            special education. The ballot says “these caps” don’t affect classroom instruction, but a co-sponsor, Sen.
            Dan Innis, told the Keene Sentinel that sentence covers only the 6% cap.
            <Cite id="rsa-32-5-i" label="The law" /> <Cite id="keene-innis" />
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <strong>It doesn’t cut anyone’s tax bill.</strong> It limits how fast the school share of property taxes
            can grow, to inflation plus new construction. Town, county, and state taxes aren’t capped, so your bill can
            still rise. <Cite id="nhpr-explainer" /> <Cite id="nhjournal-poll" />
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <strong>Farmington’s own estimate: about $360,000 short in year one.</strong> Assuming 3% inflation,
            district leaders estimate the cap would let school property taxes grow about $377,500 for 2027–28. Expected
            increases in staff pay and health insurance (projected to rise 19%) total about $738,300. That’s before special education,
            fuel, and transportation. <Cite id="school-board-video" href={vid(2772)} label="School Board video" />
            <a href="#calculator">Try other inflation numbers</a>
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <strong>Once it’s in, 41 voters can block what 59 want.</strong> Any budget that would need taxes above the
            cap must win 60% “yes.” HB 1300 doesn’t include a way to repeal it early, and the question returns in 2028.
            <Cite id="rsa-32-5-i" label="RSA 32:5-i, X" /> <Cite id="nhb-howitworks" />
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <strong>Farmington voters already have the final say.</strong> The school budget goes to a town-wide ballot
            every March. In 2026, voters rejected the proposed budget, 239–337, and a smaller default budget took
            effect. <Cite id="farmington-special-warrant-2026" /> <Cite id="farmington-results-2026" />
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
      <h3 class="yesno-title">What your vote does</h3>
      <div class="grid two yesno">
        <div class="card choice">
          <p class="choice-label"><span class="oval filled" aria-hidden="true"></span>If 60% or more vote Yes</p>
          <p>
            Starting with the 2027–28 budget, Farmington’s school property-tax levy can grow only by inflation plus new
            construction, and going over that takes a 60% vote. Central-office spending is capped at 6% of the budget.
            <Cite id="rsa-32-5-i" label="RSA 32:5-i, VIII–XII" />
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card choice">
          <p class="choice-label"><span class="oval" aria-hidden="true"></span>If fewer than 60% vote Yes</p>
          <p>
            No new cap. Farmington keeps deciding its school budget the way it does now, on the March town ballot. The
            question comes back in November 2028 either way.
            <Cite id="rsa-32-5-i" label="RSA 32:5-i, VI" /> <Cite id="nhb-howitworks" />
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="callout prose">
        <p>
          <strong>In fairness:</strong> supporters’ best case is that property taxes have climbed fast and that caps
          elsewhere have slowed tax growth. I think that in Farmington, where total school spending has grown more slowly
          than prices (though faster per student, as enrollment fell) and voters already say no when they want to, the risk to students is the bigger concern. <a href="#other-side">Read their arguments</a>
        </p>
      </div>
      <p class="prose" style="margin:1.25rem 0 0"><strong>Know someone voting in Farmington?</strong> Send them the 1-minute version.</p>
      <ShareRow />
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ===================== FARMINGTON ===================== -->
  <section id="farmington">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">What it means for Farmington</p>
      <h2>Farmington’s own estimate: about $360,000 short in year one</h2>
      <p class="lede">
        At the October 19 School Board meeting, district leaders walked through what the cap would mean for the 2027–28
        budget, the first one it would cover.
      </p>

      <div class="split">
        <div class="prose district-estimate">
          <p>
            Assuming 3% inflation and little new construction, they estimate the cap would let school property taxes
            grow about <strong>$377,500</strong>. Expected increases for staff contracts, health insurance (projected
            to rise 19%), and dental come to about <strong>$738,300</strong>.
            <Cite id="school-board-video" href={vid(2603)} label="Video, 43:23" />
            <Cite id="school-board-video" href={vid(2717)} label="45:17" />
          </p>
          <p class="gap-line">
            That leaves a gap of about <strong>$360,000</strong>. And it doesn’t yet count special education,
            out-of-district placements (students the district pays to send to other schools), utilities, fuel, or
            transportation. <Cite id="school-board-video" href={vid(2772)} label="Video, 46:12" />
            <Cite id="school-board-video" href={vid(2960)} label="49:20" />
          </p>
          <p>
            Their conclusion: it’s “pretty safe to say should this tax cap be put into place … that we’ll fall short.”
            <Cite id="school-board-video" href={vid(3754)} label="1:02:34" />
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card calc-card" id="calculator">
          <p class="calc-title">What would the cap allow in 2027–28?</p>
          <CapCalculator>
            <p class="calc-note small muted">
              Uses the district’s preliminary figures: a starting school levy of about $12.58 million and $738,287 in
              expected increases, before special education, fuel, and transportation. The cap adds inflation and new
              construction to last year’s levy; the result is my math. By my estimate, new construction has added under
              1% a year in Farmington outside revaluation years. <Cite id="school-board-video" href={vid(2772)} label="Video" />
              <Cite id="rsa-32-5-i" label="RSA 32:5-i, VIII" />
            </p>
          </CapCalculator>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="prose">
        <h3>Why so tight?</h3>
        <p>
          The 3% applies only to money raised from local property taxes. The rest of Farmington’s $18.1 million school
          budget is paid for by state aid, federal grants, and other revenue, which the cap doesn’t touch. So by the
          district’s numbers, if that other money stays flat, the cap would let the budget grow about 2%, while expected
          costs rise about 4%.
          <Cite id="farmington-special-warrant-2026" label="2026–27 budget" />
        </p>
        <p class="small muted">
          These are the district’s preliminary estimates, and they could move either way. The 2% and 4% are my math from
          the district’s figures. Two things aren’t settled. Recent Northeast inflation readings (3.6% in March, 4.2% in
          September) are above 3%, which would shrink the gap. <Cite id="bls-cpi-ne" /> But the district started from a
          tax figure of about $12.6 million, while the state’s tax-rate sheet put Farmington’s local school tax for
          2025–26 at about $7.9 million, after state aid. <Cite id="town-report-2025" label="2025 tax rate sheet" /> The
          2026–27 figure, which the cap would build on, will be set this fall. If it is closer to the state’s figure, 3%
          would allow only about $236,000, and the gap would be closer to $500,000 (my math).
        </p>
        <blockquote class="quote">
          “I worry that should this go to the ballot, people will believe that if it’s implemented, it won’t have a
          negative impact on students.”
          <footer>
            Superintendent Elizabeth McAndrews, Farmington School Board, Sept. 14, 2026
            <Cite id="school-board-video-0914" label="Video, 35:14" />
          </footer>
        </blockquote>

        <p class="sub-stat" aria-hidden="true"><b>10%</b> less buying power than in 2016</p>
        <h3 id="spending">Has Farmington’s spending kept up with inflation?</h3>
        <p class="takeaway">
          No. Farmington’s total school spending grew 20.7% from 2016 to 2025, while prices rose 34.1%.
        </p>
        <SpendChart />
        <p>
          The district spent about $16.5 million in fiscal 2016 (the school year ending June 2016) and about
          $20.0 million in fiscal 2025, according to its annual financial reports to the state. No audit of fiscal 2025
          has been published yet. <Cite id="nhdoe-doe25" /> That total counts spending from every fund, so it isn’t the
          same as the operating budget voters approve each March (for 2026–27, the $18.1 million default). Consumer prices rose 34.1% over the same years. <Cite id="bls-cpi-us" />
          By my math, buying what $16.5 million bought in 2016 would have taken about $22.2 million in 2025, about
          $2.2 million more than the district spent. Census Bureau figures tell the same story: in every year from 2017
          through 2024, spending stayed below the 2016 level adjusted for inflation, though in some years it grew
          faster than prices. <Cite id="census-f33-tables" />
        </p>
        <p>
          In fairness, enrollment fell about a quarter over the same period, from 991 students to 738.
          <Cite id="nces-enrollment-farmington" /> So spending per student rose faster than prices. By the district’s
          totals above, it rose about 62% from 2016 to 2025 (my math), while prices rose 34%. Census figures, which count
          only day-to-day spending and allow a statewide comparison but stop at 2024, show a 43% rise from 2016 to 2024,
          against about 31% for prices and 50% for the statewide average. In 2024 Farmington spent about the state
          average per student, and less than about 7 in 10 New Hampshire school districts. (The statewide average is
          weighted toward larger districts, which tend to spend less per student.)
          <Cite id="census-f33-2016" /> <Cite id="census-f33-2024" /> <Cite id="census-f33-2016-states" />
          <Cite id="census-f33-2024-states" /> Many costs don’t shrink when enrollment does, like buildings, bus
          routes, and special education.
        </p>
        <p>
          One caution about the starting year. In 2015–16 Farmington was still paid about $1.4 million in tuition to
          teach students from Middleton, which had left SAU 61 in 2015 and begun sending its students in grades 7–12 to
          another district. That tuition was nearly gone the next year. <Cite id="farmington-audit-2016" />
          <Cite id="census-f33-2016" /> <Cite id="census-f33-tables" /> So some of the drop in enrollment and spending
          after 2016 reflects fewer tuition students, not cuts. And when SAU 61’s central office became part of the
          Farmington district in July 2016, some administrative costs moved between categories.
          <Cite id="farmington-audit-2016" />
        </p>
        <p>
          Special-education costs have jumped recently. In the district’s budget reports, spending on special-education
          programs in the operating budget rose from about $2.8 million in 2023–24 to about $4.0 million in 2024–25, an
          increase the 2024–25 budget had planned for.
          <Cite id="farmington-ms27-2025" /> <Cite id="farmington-ms27-2026" /> Over the longer run, most areas have
          lost buying power since 2016. Support for teachers, like curriculum work, training, and library services,
          lost the most; business and central services grew. <Cite id="census-f33-2016" /> <Cite id="census-f33-2024" />
        </p>
        <details>
          <summary>Spending by category, 2016 to 2024, adjusted for inflation</summary>
          <div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-labelledby="spend-cap">
            <table class="spend-table">
              <caption id="spend-cap" class="sr-only">Farmington school spending by category, fiscal 2016 and 2024</caption>
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="col">Category</th>
                  <th scope="col" class="num">After inflation</th>
                  <th scope="col" class="num">2016</th>
                  <th scope="col" class="num">2024</th>
                  <th scope="col" class="num">Dollar change</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {spendingCategories.rows.map((r) => (
                  <tr>
                    <th scope="row">{r.label}{r.note && <span class="muted small"> ({r.note})</span>}</th>
                    <td class="num"><strong>{spendRow(r).real}</strong></td>
                    <td class="num">{thousands(r.fy2016)}</td>
                    <td class="num">{thousands(r.fy2024)}</td>
                    <td class="num">{spendRow(r).nominal}</td>
                  </tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
              <tfoot>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">{spendingCategories.total.label}</th>
                  <td class="num"><strong>{spendRow(spendingCategories.total).real}</strong></td>
                  <td class="num">{thousands(spendingCategories.total.fy2016)}</td>
                  <td class="num">{thousands(spendingCategories.total.fy2024)}</td>
                  <td class="num">{spendRow(spendingCategories.total).nominal}</td>
                </tr>
              </tfoot>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p class="small muted">
            “After inflation” is the change in buying power, not necessarily a cut in dollars: a category that grew
            less than 30.7%, the rise in consumer prices from 2016 to 2024, buys less than it did. Instruction includes
            special education; the Census Bureau doesn’t report it separately. Some shifts may reflect how costs were
            recorded rather than real changes: general administration fell while business and central services grew.
            The Census Bureau hasn’t published 2025 yet. The total is day-to-day spending; it leaves out construction,
            equipment, and debt payments. That’s why it shows a bigger loss of buying power (16%) than the 10% above: it
            stops at 2024, before spending jumped in fiscal 2025, and counts only day-to-day costs.
            <Cite id="census-f33-2016" /> <Cite id="census-f33-2024" /> <Cite id="bls-cpi-us" />
          </p>
        </details>

        <div class="callout">
          <p>
            <strong>Spending, property taxes, and state aid are three different numbers.</strong> Spending is what the
            district spends to run its schools. The school property tax is the part Farmington residents raise
            locally. State aid is what the state contributes, which affects how much is left for local taxpayers.
            These can move in different directions: property taxes can rise even while spending, adjusted for
            inflation, falls, if state aid and other money don’t keep up. In Farmington, Census Bureau figures show
            that from 2016 to 2024, school property taxes rose about 30%, roughly in step with prices, while state
            money (which in Census figures includes the statewide education property tax) fell about 3% before
            inflation, as enrollment fell and most state aid is paid per student. Day-to-day spending, the Census
            measure in the table above, rose about 10%. <Cite id="census-f33-2016" /> <Cite id="census-f33-2024" />
            <Cite id="rsa-198-40-a" /> That’s why I don’t think a cap on the local
            tax gets at the real problem. It limits what Farmington can raise, but it doesn’t lower what it costs to
            educate students, or change how those costs are split between the state and towns.
            <a href="#nh-context">How New Hampshire splits the cost</a> · <a href="#alternatives">What else could help</a>
          </p>
        </div>

        <p class="sub-stat" aria-hidden="true"><b>11</b> full-time positions lost and not replaced</p>
        <h3 id="cuts">Where would cuts come from?</h3>
        <p class="takeaway">
          No specific cuts have been proposed. Special-education services can’t be cut, district leaders said, so cuts
          would fall on regular education: staff, the biggest cost, and programs that aren’t graduation requirements,
          like athletics and theater. <Cite id="school-board-video" href={vid(4161)} label="Video, 1:09:21" />
          <Cite id="school-board-video" href={vid(3790)} label="1:03:10" />
        </p>
        <p>
          District leaders said Farmington has lost about 11 full-time positions over the last couple of years that
          weren’t replaced. The high school has no music teacher of its own (another staff member covers), and one
          librarian covers two schools. “I
          don’t know how thin we can spread that,” one told the board. “Everyone’s doing extra jobs at this point.”
          <Cite id="school-board-video" href={vid(3920)} label="Video, 1:05:20" />
          <Cite id="school-board-video" href={vid(3992)} label="1:06:32" />
        </p>
        <p>
          Why not special education? Each student’s written plan, called an IEP, “is a legal document and what is
          promised in that document has to be delivered,” a district leader said.
          <Cite id="school-board-video" href={vid(4161)} label="Video, 1:09:21" />
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Some costs aren’t optional.</strong> Federal law requires the district to provide the services written
          into each special-education student’s plan, and the U.S. Supreme Court has ruled that a district must fund a
          service a student needs in order to attend school, even when it’s costly. <Cite id="idea-1414" label="IDEA, 20 U.S.C. 1414(d)" />
          <Cite id="garret-f" /> Federal rules also generally bar a district from spending less on special education than
          the year before, with exceptions such as fewer students needing services.
          <Cite id="cfr-300-203" label="34 CFR 300.203" /> And state rules set a floor every school must meet to keep its
          approval: instruction in the core subjects plus the arts, health, and physical education (and computer science
          from middle school up), along with licensed school counselors and library media services. <Cite id="ed-306" href="https://www.law.cornell.edu/regulations/new-hampshire/N-H-Admin-Code-SS-Ed-306.21" label="NH rules Ed 306.21" />
          <Cite id="ed-306" href="https://www.law.cornell.edu/regulations/new-hampshire/N-H-Admin-Code-SS-Ed-306.12" label="Ed 306.12" /> <Cite id="rsa-193-e-2-a" /> The cap makes no exception for
          any of these costs. Only state-approved building-bond payments are left out.
          <Cite id="rsa-32-5-i" label="RSA 32:5-i, VIII" />
        </p>
        <p>
          That doesn’t mean every course or job is protected. School boards decide how to meet the standards. They can
          offer some required high school courses online or through regional technical centers, or ask the state to
          approve another way of complying. <Cite id="rsa-193-e-2-a" label="RSA 193-E:2-a, V" />
          <Cite id="ed-306" href="https://www.law.cornell.edu/regulations/new-hampshire/N-H-Admin-Code-SS-Ed-306.22" label="Ed 306.22" /> <Cite id="ed-306" href="https://www.law.cornell.edu/regulations/new-hampshire/N-H-Admin-Code-SS-Ed-306.25" label="Ed 306.25" />
          So reductions would fall hardest on what the district provides above those minimums, such as extra staff,
          electives, sports, and clubs, which is where district leaders said they would look.
          <Cite id="school-board-video" href={vid(3790)} label="Video, 1:03:10" />
        </p>
        <p>
          This year, at the January deliberative session, the district had to correct its fallback (“default”) budget,
          adding $700,000 for health insurance increases it was obligated to pay.
          <Cite id="farmington-ar-2026" label="Deliberative session minutes" />
        </p>

        <p class="sub-stat" aria-hidden="true"><b>5.7%</b> central-office share, about $47,000 under the 6% limit</p>
        <h3>Would the 6% administration limit help here?</h3>
        <p>
          <strong>Probably not much, though the margin is thin.</strong> By the district’s count, central-office costs
          come to about $991,000, about 5.7% of the budget and roughly $47,000 under the 6% limit. The superintendent
          said some costs, like the district’s IT administrator and part of its legal bills, could land on either side.
          <Cite id="school-board-video" href={vid(3512)} label="Video, 58:32" /> And because the limit is a share of the
          budget, it would shrink if the levy cap forced the budget down. For now, the levy cap is the part that would
          bite. <Cite id="school-board-video" href={vid(3588)} label="Video, 59:48" />
          <Cite id="school-board-video" href={vid(3749)} label="1:02:29" />
        </p>

        <p class="sub-stat" aria-hidden="true"><b>239–337</b> the 2026 school budget vote</p>
        <h3>Don’t voters already decide?</h3>
        <p>
          Farmington votes on its school budget by ballot every March, and voters use that power. In 2026 the proposed
          budget on the ballot was $19.4 million, which the ballot estimated would raise the school tax rate by $3.25 per
          $1,000 of property value. Voters said no, 239–337, and the $18.1 million default budget took effect. They also rejected the
          teachers’ contract, 264–323. <Cite id="farmington-ballot-2026" /> <Cite id="farmington-special-warrant-2026" />
          <Cite id="farmington-results-2026" /> Even so, the default budget was about 4% above the year before, mostly
          for health insurance the district was obligated to pay. <Cite id="farmington-default-budget-2026" />
        </p>
        <p>
          In 2025, voters passed the budget 308–100 and turned down a petitioned school tax cap, 185–228. (The
          deliberative session had raised that cap’s yearly limit from $250,000 to $1 million before the vote.)
          <Cite id="farmington-results-2025" /> <Cite id="farmington-ar-2025" label="Deliberative session minutes" />
        </p>

        <p class="sub-stat" aria-hidden="true"><b>2023</b> audit returned without an opinion</p>
        <h3>What about the district’s own money troubles?</h3>
        <p>
          Farmington’s school finances have had real problems, and it’s fair to say so. The district’s 2023 audit came
          back without an opinion, meaning the auditors couldn’t say whether the books were accurate, because
          “inadequate or no documentation exists to support material account balances and transactions.”
          <Cite id="farmington-audit-2023" />
        </p>
        <p>
          In 2025–26 the district ran short of money. The board chair called the shortfall the year’s “most frustrating
          challenge,” said the board had made “significant changes to our business department” to strengthen financial
          management, and said those challenges, “coupled with unanticipated healthcare bills,” led to a mid-year
          Reduction in Force: the district’s first layoffs in years, along with a spending freeze.
          <Cite id="farmington-ar-2026" label="Farmington annual report, pp. 29–30" /> The district has taken steps to
          address its financial management challenges, including hiring a new business administrator scheduled to begin
          in late October 2026. <Cite id="school-board-video" href={vid(395)} label="Video, 6:35" />
        </p>
        <p>
          Cap supporters can fairly point to this as a reason voters want firmer limits. My view: a tax cap didn’t cause
          that shortfall, and a cap won’t fix bookkeeping. Under a cap, any rebuilding above the limit would need a 60%
          vote.
        </p>

        <p style="margin-top:1.5rem">
          Hear it from the people who build the budget: the district’s presentation on HB 1300 at the October 19 School
          Board meeting runs about 35 minutes, with questions from residents. <Cite id="school-board-video" label="Watch from 37:21" />
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ===================== OUTCOMES ===================== -->
  <section id="outcomes" class="alt">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">Student outcomes</p>
      <h2>Farmington students were closing the gap with the state</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="takeaway">
          Farmington worked to narrow the gap between its students and the state average, and between 2021 and 2024 it
          closed most of it. In 2026, much of that gap reopened. That progress, and getting it back, matters, especially
          in a community facing real economic challenges.
        </p>
        <p>
          Farmington students have long scored below the state average. Between 2021 and 2024 they closed most of that
          gap in math and reading, and in science they edged past the state in 2022 (by 1 point) and beat it in 2023 and
          2025. <Cite id="farmington-ar-2025" label="Farmington annual report" /> <Cite id="sdganh-scores" />
        </p>
        <p>
          I believe keeping that progress takes steady support. In spring 2026, after a school year with a spending
          freeze and the district’s first layoffs in years, results fell: reading went from 49% to 41% of students
          scoring proficient or better, math from 33% to 31%, and science from 45% to 26%, while state averages held
          steady or rose. <Cite id="sdganh-scores" /> <Cite id="keene-2026-scores" />
          <Cite id="farmington-ar-2026" label="Farmington annual report, pp. 29–30" /> Math had already slipped a year
          earlier, from 39% to 33% in spring 2025, before the freeze and layoffs. That doesn’t show the layoffs caused
          the drop: in 2026 Farmington tested only about 320 students in math and reading and about 140 in science, each
          year tests different students, and many things affect scores. But the decline deserves a close look before
          new limits on school funding.
        </p>
        <p class="small muted">
          Choose a subject to see Farmington and New Hampshire side by side. Each point is the share of students scoring
          proficient or better on that spring’s state tests: math and reading in grades 3–8 plus the grade 11 SAT, and
          science in grades 5, 8, and 11. There were no state tests in 2020, and the grade 11 SAT changed format in 2024.
          Results for each grade and test are below the chart.
        </p>
      </div>
      <ScoreChart />
      <div class="prose">
        <details>
