  <!-- ===================== COMMUNITY ===================== -->
  <section id="community">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">Families and taxpayers</p>
      <h2>Farmington families have less room to absorb rising costs</h2>
      <div class="stat-tiles">
        <div class="stat-tile">
          <p class="stat-num">$66,943</p>
          <p class="stat-label">median household income in Farmington, about two-thirds of the state’s $99,031</p>
          <p class="stat-src"><Cite id="acs-income-farmington" /></p>
        </div>
        <div class="stat-tile">
          <p class="stat-num">11%</p>
          <p class="stat-label">of residents below the poverty line, vs. 7.2% statewide (Farmington’s estimate: ±5.6 points)</p>
          <p class="stat-src"><Cite id="acs-poverty-farmington" /></p>
        </div>
        <div class="stat-tile">
          <p class="stat-num">11%</p>
          <p class="stat-label">of the school district’s children ages 5–17 in poverty in 2024, vs. 6% statewide</p>
          <p class="stat-src"><Cite id="saipe-2024" /></p>
        </div>
        <div class="stat-tile">
          <p class="stat-num">4 in 10</p>
          <p class="stat-label">students qualify for free or reduced-price lunch, vs. about 1 in 4 statewide</p>
          <p class="stat-src"><Cite id="nces-lunch-farmington" /></p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="takeaway">Farmington faces challenges that go beyond the school budget.</p>
        <p>
          About 11% of Farmington residents lived below the poverty line in 2020–2024, compared with 7.2% statewide,
          roughly one and a half times the state rate. <Cite id="acs-poverty-farmington" /> <Cite id="acs-poverty-nh" />
          Farmington’s figure comes from a small survey sample (plus or minus 5.6 points), so the exact gap is uncertain,
          but other measures point the same way. A Census Bureau model estimates that about 11% of the school
          district’s children ages 5 to 17 were in poverty in 2024, compared with 6% statewide.
          <Cite id="saipe-2024" /> And Farmington’s median household income, $66,943, is about two-thirds of the state’s
          $99,031. <Cite id="acs-income-farmington" /> <Cite id="acs-income-nh" />
        </p>
        <p>
          So many families have less room to absorb rising costs or to pay for extras like tutoring, and more children
          rely on what school provides: about 4 in 10 Farmington students qualify for free or reduced-price lunch,
          compared with about 1 in 4 statewide. <Cite id="nces-lunch-farmington" /> <Cite id="nces-lunch-nh" /> The
          state puts Farmington in its top school building aid tier, reserved for “less affluent communities.”
          <Cite id="nhed-building-aid" />
        </p>

        <h3>Why schools matter here</h3>
        <p>
          I believe a good public education is one of the most important tools a community has to expand opportunity
          and break the cycle of poverty, and the research backs that up. A major study of school-finance reforms found
          that 10% more spending per student throughout a child’s school years led to more years of school, higher
          adult wages, and less adult poverty. For children from low-income families, it cut the chance of being poor in
          a given adult year by about 6 percentage points. <Cite id="jjp-2016" /> A review of U.S. studies of school
          spending found that
          $1,000 more per student for four years raised test scores and college-going, especially for students from
          lower-income families. <Cite id="jackson-mackevicius-2024" /> And when school budgets were cut during the Great
          Recession, test scores and college-going fell, and gaps by income and race widened.
          <Cite id="jackson-wigger-xiong-2021" />
        </p>
        <p>
          That’s why I worry that limiting what schools can offer, in a community already facing economic hardship,
          risks deepening those gaps. In fairness, how money is spent matters, and some economists argue that more
          resources alone have done little. <Cite id="hanushek-2003" /> A 2021 re-analysis of the Great Recession study,
          not peer-reviewed, also questioned how much those cuts lowered test scores and college-going.
          <Cite id="goldstein-mcgee-2021" />
        </p>

        <h3>Property taxes are a real burden, too</h3>
        <p>
          New Hampshire relies on property taxes more than any other state: they make up about 60% of state and local
          tax revenue, and the effective tax rate on homes is the 5th highest in the country.
          <Cite id="taxfoundation-proptax-2026" /> That weighs on residents with fixed incomes. Statewide, about 3 in 10
          homeowners 65 and older spend 30% or more of their income on housing costs, including property taxes.
          <Cite id="acs-owner-costs-nh" /> With Farmington’s lower incomes, tax increases hit harder here, and that’s a
          real reason some neighbors support a cap. <a href="#alternatives">What else could help</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ===================== BALLOT ===================== -->
  <section id="ballot" class="alt">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">Read it closely</p>
      <h2>What the ballot says vs. what the law does</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="lede">
          Here is Question 2 exactly as it appears on the back of Farmington’s ballot. Look at the highlighted sentence.
        </p>
        <figure>
          <div class="ballot">
            <div class="q">Statutory question required by HB 1300, Chapter 324, 2026</div>
            <p><strong>2.</strong> {ballotText.before}<mark><span class="sr-only">Highlighted sentence: </span>{ballotText.marked}<span class="sr-only"> (end of highlight)</span></mark>{ballotText.after}</p>
            <div class="choices" aria-hidden="true"><span><span class="oval"></span>Yes</span><span><span class="oval"></span>No</span></div>
          </div>
          <figcaption>
            Replica of Question 2 as printed on Farmington’s ballot, with the classroom sentence highlighted. Wording from
            the <Cite id="farmington-sample-ballot" label="Town of Farmington sample ballot" />. State law sets
            the same wording for every town. <Cite id="rsa-32-5-i" label="RSA 32:5-i, V" /> Key: SAU = the superintendent’s
            office · appropriations = what voters approve to spend · bonded capital costs = loan payments on school
            buildings · overridden = voted over · three-fifths = 60%.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>

      <h3 class="claims-title">The ballot says one thing. Check it against the law.</h3>
      <div class="grid two claims">
        <article class="card claim">
          <p class="claim-label">The ballot says</p>
          <p class="claim-quote">“These caps apply only to administrative operations of the SAU central office and do not affect classroom instruction, school-based services…”</p>
          <button type="button" class="reveal-btn" data-reveal="classroom" aria-controls="law-classroom" hidden>See what the law says</button>
          <div class="claim-law" id="law-classroom">
            <p class="claim-label">The law itself says</p>
            <p>
              Only the 6% administration cap excludes classroom instruction. The other cap is on the district’s whole
              property-tax levy, “the estimated amount of local taxes to be raised for the fiscal year attributable to the
              school district,” which helps pay for the entire school budget. If a budget would need more tax money than
              the cap allows, the state “shall not approve any tax rate that exceeds the certified limit” unless 60% of
              voters approve going over it. The law doesn’t say which parts of the budget would be cut.
              <Cite id="rsa-32-5-i" label="RSA 32:5-i, VIII, IX, XI & XV" />
            </p>
          </div>
        </article>
        <article class="card claim">
          <p class="claim-label">The ballot says</p>
          <p class="claim-quote">“limit property tax growth”</p>
          <button type="button" class="reveal-btn" data-reveal="tax-growth" aria-controls="law-tax-growth" hidden>See what the law says</button>
          <div class="claim-law" id="law-tax-growth">
            <p class="claim-label">The law itself says</p>
            <p>
              It limits only the school district’s share. It doesn’t limit town, county, or state education taxes, so
              your own tax bill can still go up. <Cite id="nhpr-explainer" />
            </p>
          </div>
        </article>
      </div>

      <div class="grid two" style="margin-top:1.5rem">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Tax cap or school cap?</h3>
          <p>
            It does cap a tax: the total the school district can raise in property taxes. But both of its limits apply
            only to the school district, and it is <strong>not</strong> a cap on your tax bill or your tax rate. It
            doesn’t cut anyone’s current bill. At most it slows how fast the school portion can grow, and it still lets
            the school levy grow with inflation and new construction. Even NH Journal, a right-leaning news site, wrote
            that it “would not guarantee a particular limit on an individual homeowner’s bill.”
            <Cite id="nhjournal-poll" /> <Cite id="rsa-32-5-i" label="RSA 32:5-i, XV" />
          </p>
          <p>
            Supporters call it the “Taxpayer Protection Act.” <Cite id="housegop-signing" /> A more precise name would be
            a <strong>cap on local school funding</strong>: a school cap.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>The other side, in fairness</h3>
          <p>
            Rep. Ross Berry, a Republican from Weare who proposed the tax-cap amendment and helped write the ballot
            question, says the classroom sentence is accurate because the 6% limit is “the only spending cap that exists.”
            <Cite id="vnews-wording" /> <Cite id="nhb-origin" label="His amendment" /> He argues that because the cap allows
            for inflation, classroom teaching should not be affected. <Cite id="monitor-confusion" />
          </p>
          <p>
            Two taxpayers sued, saying the question is confusing and misleading and bundles two caps into one vote.
            <Cite id="bates-complaint" /> <Cite id="nhb-lawsuit" /> The Superior Court refused to block it, finding they
            hadn’t shown they were likely to win and calling their evidence of classroom effects “speculative and
            uncertain.” <Cite id="superior-order" /> The NH Supreme Court also declined to step in.
            <Cite id="nhsc-order-0917" /> <Cite id="nhpr-court" /> <Cite id="indepthnh-supreme" />
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ===================== 60% ===================== -->
  <section id="sixty">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">The 60% rule</p>
      <h2>Once it’s in, 41 voters can block what 59 want</h2>
      <div class="grid two" style="align-items:center">
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            If Question 2 passes in Farmington, any school budget that would need property taxes above the limit must win
            <strong>60% “yes”</strong> on the ballot. A simple majority isn’t enough.
            <Cite id="rsa-32-5-i" label="RSA 32:5-i, X(a)" />
          </p>
          <p>
            If it falls short, existing law sends Farmington to its default budget, and under HB 1300 the state still
            would not approve a school tax rate above the cap. HB 1300 doesn’t say how default budgets fit under the cap.
            <Cite id="rsa-40-13" /> <Cite id="rsa-32-5-i" label="RSA 32:5-i, XI" />
          </p>
          <p>
            HB 1300 doesn’t include a way to repeal a cap early. The question comes back on the November 2028 ballot either
            way.
            <Cite id="nhb-howitworks" />
          </p>
        </div>
        <VoteSlider />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ===================== OTHER SIDE ===================== -->
  <section id="other-side" class="alt">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">The other side</p>
      <h2>What supporters say, and what to weigh</h2>
      <p class="lede">
        People backing Question 2 have real concerns about the cost of living. Here are their strongest arguments,
        stated fairly, and the facts I think belong next to them.
      </p>
      <div class="args">
        <article class="card arg">
          <div class="arg-for">
            <p class="arg-label">Supporters say</p>
            <h3>Property taxes are climbing too fast.</h3>
            <p>
              Statewide, local property taxes rose 47.5% from
              2016 to 2025, or about 12% after inflation. <Cite id="nhfpi-proptax" />
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="arg-weigh">
            <p class="arg-label">Worth weighing</p>
            <p>
              True, and it hurts. But New Hampshire’s state government pays a smaller share of school costs than any
              other state, so more of each increase lands on local property taxes. Question 2 doesn’t change that. It
              only limits the local share. <Cite id="nhb-state-share" />
            </p>
          </div>
        </article>
        <article class="card arg">
          <div class="arg-for">
            <p class="arg-label">Supporters say</p>
            <h3>It isn’t a cut.</h3>
            <p>
              The levy can still grow every year with inflation plus new construction.
              Rep. Ross Berry: “I would love to know how a 3.9% property tax increase results in them having to cut
              anything.” <Cite id="vnews-wording" />
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="arg-weigh">
            <p class="arg-label">Worth weighing</p>
            <p>
              In Farmington, property taxes pay only part of the school budget. By my math from the district’s
              figures, even a 3.9% levy increase (about $490,000) works out to under 3% budget growth, short of the
              roughly $738,000 (about 4%) in expected cost increases, mostly contracts and a projected 19%
              health-insurance increase. <Cite id="school-board-video" href={vid(2772)} label="School Board video" />
            </p>
          </div>
        </article>
        <article class="card arg">
          <div class="arg-for">
            <p class="arg-label">Supporters say</p>
            <h3>Enrollment is falling while spending rises.</h3>
            <p>
              New Hampshire public schools had 160,323
              students in fall 2025, down from more than 205,000 twenty years earlier. <Cite id="keene-enrollment" />
              A free-market research group found spending per student nearly doubled from 2001 to 2024, after
              inflation. <Cite id="jbc-spending" />
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="arg-weigh">
            <p class="arg-label">Worth weighing</p>
            <p>
              AFT-NH, the teachers union, notes that schools still have fixed costs like running buses and heating
              classrooms, and that it’s hard to cut staff when fewer students are spread across many grades.
              <Cite id="keene-enrollment" /> In Farmington, total school spending grew more slowly than
              prices. Per student it rose 43%, faster than prices but slower than the state’s 50%.
              <a href="#spending">See Farmington’s numbers</a>
            </p>
          </div>
        </article>
        <article class="card arg">
          <div class="arg-for">
            <p class="arg-label">Supporters say</p>
            <h3>A November vote lets more people decide.</h3>
            <p>
              Early-2025 votes on school-spending caps in eight
              districts drew about 20% as many voters as the 2024 presidential election. <Cite id="monitor-caps-2025" />
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="arg-weigh">
            <p class="arg-label">Worth weighing</p>
            <p>
              That’s a fair point. But once a cap is in place, every vote to go over it happens at Farmington’s regular
              March school election, not in November. <Cite id="rsa-32-5-i" label="RSA 32:5-i, X(a)" />
            </p>
          </div>
        </article>
        <article class="card arg">
          <div class="arg-for">
            <p class="arg-label">Supporters say</p>
            <h3>It puts money in classrooms, not administration.</h3>
            <p>
              Americans for Prosperity-NH says the law
              “ensures education dollars are spent in the classroom, not on administrative overhead.”
              <Cite id="keene-innis" /> Statewide, administrator positions grew 78% from 2001 to 2024 while teacher
              positions grew 1%. <Cite id="jbc-spending" />
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="arg-weigh">
            <p class="arg-label">Worth weighing</p>
            <p>
              Farmington’s central office already runs under 6%. The superintendent said it has “not a lot of people”
              and no communications staff: “a big fat zero for us.”
              <Cite id="school-board-video" href={vid(3537)} label="School Board video" /> And studies of older caps in
              other states found no sign that administration absorbed the cuts: in Oregon, administration’s share of
              spending held steady or rose while class sizes grew. <Cite id="figlio-1997" /> <Cite id="figlio-1998" /> A
              newer New York study found cuts spread across instruction, central administration, and transportation.
              <Cite id="nguyen-hoang-2022" />
            </p>
          </div>
        </article>
        <article class="card arg">
          <div class="arg-for">
            <p class="arg-label">Supporters say</p>
            <h3>Caps do slow tax growth.</h3>
            <p>
              After New York capped school levies, average school tax
              increases fell to 1.9% a year in 2012–2018, from 6.6% in 2004–2008 and 3.1% in the recession years
              just before the cap took effect. More than 95% of school budgets still passed each year. <Cite id="rockinst-ny" />
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="arg-weigh">
            <p class="arg-label">Worth weighing</p>
            <p>
              That’s the trade-off. A study of New York’s cap found that each $1,000 per student a district lost to it
              lowered test scores, driven by cuts to instruction, teacher hiring, and support staff.
              <Cite id="sorensen-2021" />
            </p>
          </div>
        </article>
        <article class="card arg">
          <div class="arg-for">
            <p class="arg-label">Supporters say</p>
            <h3>Most states limit property taxes, and Massachusetts has had a tougher cap since 1980.</h3>
            <p>
              New Hampshire is one of only seven states with no statewide limit. <Cite id="jbc-firstchance" /> HB 1300’s
              cap is tied to inflation, so it never allows less growth than inflation. <Cite id="jbc-massway" />
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="arg-weigh">
            <p class="arg-label">Worth weighing</p>
            <p>
              Massachusetts’ state government pays about 44% of school funding, compared with 28% in New Hampshire.
              <Cite id="newamerica-ma" /> <Cite id="newamerica-nh" /> And Massachusetts towns can go over their cap with
              a simple majority of voters, once local officials put it on the ballot. HB 1300 requires 60%.
              <Cite id="ma-primer" />
            </p>
          </div>
        </article>
      </div>

      <div class="grid two" style="margin-top:1.25rem">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>What research on tax caps has found</h3>
          <p>
            A 49-state study of older, mostly stricter tax limits found they were linked to more students per teacher,
            lower starting teacher pay, and lower test scores, with no sign of lower administrative costs.
            <Cite id="figlio-1997" /> A study of Oregon’s limit found a similar pattern. <Cite id="figlio-1998" /> Other
            studies found little effect on student performance. <Cite id="ddm-1998" />
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Doubts about caps cross party lines</h3>
          <p>
            In January 2026, 22 House Republicans joined Democrats to narrow a statewide school budget cap bill (HB 675)
            to administrative spending only. Backing that change, Republican Rep. Kristine Perez said a study in her town
            had convinced her that a tax cap is not an ideal way to restrain spending. The changed bill was then killed.
            <Cite id="nhb-hb675" /> Local votes are mixed: in March 2026, voters in nine communities rejected tax-cap
            articles, while Kingston and Lebanon adopted caps. <Cite id="nhpr-rejected" />
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ===================== ALTERNATIVES ===================== -->
  <section id="alternatives">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">What about alternatives?</p>
      <h2>Property taxes are a real burden. A cap doesn’t fix the cause.</h2>
      <p class="lede">
        Farmington faces a difficult reality. Our schools need enough money to educate children, but much of that cost
        falls on local property taxpayers. For families struggling to make ends meet and older residents on fixed
        incomes, that burden can be substantial.
      </p>
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="takeaway">
          We shouldn’t have to choose between a good education for our children and letting longtime residents stay in
          their homes.
        </p>
        <p>
          In the short term, our schools need the money to meet their responsibilities. In the long term, New Hampshire
          needs to change how it pays for public education.
        </p>

        <h3>Why so much falls on property taxes</h3>
        <p>
          New Hampshire leans on local money for its schools more than any other state. In fiscal 2024, 63% of public
          school revenue came from local sources, the highest share of any state, and 28% came from the state, the
          lowest. Local property taxes alone supplied about half. <Cite id="census-f33-2024-tables" />
        </p>
        <p>
          So a town’s property wealth matters a lot. In 2023–24, Farmington had about $1.33 million of taxable property
          for each student, about two-thirds of the state average of $2.08 million; some towns have many times more.
          The state’s formula makes up part of the difference: in 2025–26, Farmington was estimated to get about
          $10,740 per student in state adequacy aid (including the state education tax it raises and keeps), against a
          statewide average of $7,109. That helps keep Farmington’s local school tax rate, once
          differences in how towns assess property are evened out, close to the state average ($7.45 per $1,000 in
          2023, vs. $7.31). <Cite id="nhed-finance-2025" /> So the challenge here isn’t an unusually high rate. It’s
          that an average rate weighs more heavily on lower incomes. <a href="#community">See Farmington’s families</a>
        </p>
        <p>
          Smaller districts can also cost more per student. A widely cited review of the research concluded that
          sizable savings may be possible when a district of 500 or fewer students grows to about 2,000 to 4,000.
          <Cite id="andrews-2002" /> <Cite id="vt-aoe-2015" /> The cost study done for the state’s 2020 school-funding
          commission found that small districts need more money per student to reach the same results. For districts
          of 601 to 1,200 students, it estimated about $2,500 more per student than for districts of more than 2,000.
          <Cite id="air-2020" label="AIR draft report, 2020" /> Farmington has about 740.
          <Cite id="nhed-finance-2025" /> Nationally, general administration (school boards and superintendents’
          offices) costs about $690 per student in districts under 3,000 students, about double the $351 average.
          <Cite id="census-f33-2024-tables" label="Census, FY2024 Table 14" /> Farmington’s came to about $855,000 in
          2024, roughly $1,100 per student (my math), including its own SAU central office since 2016.
          <Cite id="census-f33-2024" />
        </p>

        <h3>Where state funding stands</h3>
        <p>
          New Hampshire’s Supreme Court has said the state isn’t paying enough. In July 2025, in a case brought by the
          ConVal school district, it ruled 3–2 that the state’s base aid ($4,100 per student in the law; about $4,266
          in 2025–26, rising 2% a year) is unconstitutionally low, and it upheld a finding that the amount must be more
          than $7,356. A different 3–2 majority declined to order the state to pay that right away, leaving the fix to
          the Legislature and governor. <Cite id="conval-2025" /> <Cite id="rsa-198-40-a" /> <Cite id="rsa-198-40-d" />
          <Cite id="nhed-finance-2025" />
        </p>
        <p>
          Lawmakers haven’t raised base aid since, beyond the automatic 2% yearly increases. In 2026 the House and the
          Senate each killed a bill to set it at the court’s $7,356 figure. <Cite id="hb651-2026" />
          <Cite id="sb582-2026" /> The House also killed a bill that mainly would have raised special-education aid.
          <Cite id="hb1826-2026" /> Instead, a law signed in March 2026 describes New Hampshire’s public schools as “an
          integrated public education system of shared responsibility between state and local government”; its fiscal
          note says it doesn’t change aid amounts. <Cite id="hb1815-2026" /> <Cite id="hb1815-text" /> A second case,
          Rand v. State, is still open. A judge ruled in 2025 that the state’s total adequacy aid and its
          special-education aid are too low, forcing towns to cover part of the gap with local property taxes.
          <Cite id="rand-2026" /> The state has appealed, asking the Supreme Court to overturn or modify the Claremont
          decisions, the 1990s rulings behind the state’s duty to pay for an adequate education, and to leave school
          funding to lawmakers. <Cite id="monitor-rand-appeal" />
        </p>

        <h3>Ideas worth examining</h3>
        <p>
          There are possible solutions worth examining, including changes to state education funding, shared
          administration, and targeted property-tax relief for residents on fixed incomes. Each has tradeoffs.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="grid three alt-ideas">
        <article class="card">
          <h3>Change how the state pays</h3>
          <p>
            The consultants for the state’s 2020 school-funding commission found the system unfair to both students
            and taxpayers. <Cite id="air-2020" label="AIR draft report, 2020" /> The commission recommended more state
            aid aimed at districts with greater needs, including small districts, paid for by raising more money at the
            state level. The Legislature hasn’t adopted its formula. <Cite id="nhfpi-equity-2022" />
            <Cite id="rsa-198-40-a" />
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The tradeoff:</strong> that money has to come from somewhere. NH Business Review called the
            commission’s plan for a statewide property tax to pay for state aid its most controversial idea.
            <Cite id="nhbr-commission-2020" />
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <h3>Share administration</h3>
          <p>
            Combining administrative work, or even districts, deserves a look. That doesn’t have to mean merging school
            buildings or putting students in settings that aren’t right for their ages. State law already lets school
            administrative units (SAUs) merge their central offices, and pays $200 per student for two years for
            mergers completed by July 1, 2030. <Cite id="rsa-194-c-2" /> A state commission on SAU efficiency is due to
            report by Nov. 1, 2026. <Cite id="rsa-194-c-13" /> Farmington’s SAU 61 has served only Farmington since
            2016, after Middleton left. <Cite id="farmington-audit-2016" />
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The tradeoff:</strong> savings vary. In rural New York, merging two 300-student districts was
            estimated to cut costs per student about 31%, but merging two 1,500-student districts only about 14%.
            <Cite id="duncombe-yinger-2007" /> A 2021 working paper on Arkansas’s forced mergers of districts under 350
            students found no evidence of savings. <Cite id="mcgee-2021" /> And much of the savings comes from
            reorganizing schools, so merging only central offices would likely save less. <Cite id="vt-aoe-2015" />
            By the district’s estimate, Farmington’s central office is already about 5.7% of the budget.
            <Cite id="school-board-video" href={vid(3588)} label="Video, 59:48" />
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <h3>Help residents on fixed incomes</h3>
          <p>
            Farmington already offers an elderly exemption: $155,000 off a home’s assessed value at ages 65–74,
            $183,000 at 75–79, and $225,000 at 80 and older, for incomes up to $40,000 (single) or $80,000 (married) and
            assets up to $75,000, not counting the home. At the 2025 rate that’s worth roughly $2,400 to $3,500 a year
            (my math). Applications are due April 15. <Cite id="farmington-elderly" /> <Cite id="town-report-2025" />
            Residents 65 and older or with disabilities can also ask the town to defer property taxes if paying would
            cause undue hardship. Deferred taxes (up to 85% of the home’s equity) build up 5% interest and come due when
            the home is sold or transferred, or after the owner’s death. <Cite id="rsa-72-38-a" />
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Room to grow:</strong> the state’s low- and moderate-income program refunds only the state
            education tax, $1.05 of Farmington’s total $15.64 tax rate in 2025, so it pays at most about $200 here
            (my math). <Cite id="rsa-198-57" /> <Cite id="farmington-lmi" /> <Cite id="farmington-assessing" />
            Its income limits, $37,000 single and $47,000 married, haven’t changed since 2021.
            <Cite id="nhfpi-lmi-2026" /> The 2020 commission reportedly recommended
            expanding relief to renters. <Cite id="nhbr-commission-2020" /> Exemptions do shift taxes onto other
            property owners.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>

      <div class="prose">
        <p style="margin-top:1.5rem">
          These are complicated issues, and reasonable people will disagree about the best answers. Our state
          representatives have a role in building approaches that protect both students and taxpayers.
        </p>
        <p class="takeaway">
          What we can’t do is assume that limiting a school’s ability to raise money will solve the underlying problem.
          It may reduce the money available without reducing what it costs to give our children the education they
          need.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ===================== VOTE ===================== -->
